export const SERVER_INSTRUCTIONS = [
    "SendSafely is an end-to-end encrypted file exchange platform. Use these tools to send/receive encrypted packages, manage recipients, and view recipient activity related to your packages.",
    "Key workflows:",
    "- Send: create_package -> add_recipient(s) -> upload_file(s) and/or add_package_message -> finalize_package. A package is NOT delivered until finalize_package succeeds and returns a secure link.",
    "- Receive: get_received_packages -> download_package_files -> read_downloaded_file. Downloads land in a server-managed directory that may be outside your sandbox or on a different host, so the local_path the download tools return is NOT guaranteed to be openable by your own file-reading tools. To view a downloaded file's contents you MUST go through read_downloaded_file (after asking the user's permission) — it reads the file even when the path is outside your sandbox. Never try to open the path directly or treat a download whose path your file tools can't see as failed. Use list_downloaded_files to enumerate what has been downloaded rather than guessing paths. The same applies to download_file_from_directory for workspace files.",
    "- Workspaces / directories: create_workspace returns a packageId you then manage with create_directory, upload_file, download_file_from_directory, etc.",
    "Always surface the resulting secure link or status to the user.",
    "Recipient authentication options when sending: email verification is the default; choose add_recipient_with_sms for SMS-code verification; finalize_undisclosed_package gates access with a password instead of a named recipient list (do NOT also add named recipients in that case); recipients with a registered Trusted Device can also unlock packages without re-using the original secure link.",
    "Before suggesting actions on an existing package, classify it by reading `packageType` from the package info response. Call get_package_information_from_link when a link is available (this also populates the secret cache for downstream calls), otherwise get_package_information. `packageType` is the single discriminator and has exactly three values:",
    '- packageType === "WORKSPACE" (a virtual data room): supports directories (create_directory, move_directory, rename_directory), per-directory uploads/downloads (download_file_from_directory), and per-package Activity Logs. Does NOT use the flat file flow.',
    '- packageType === "TRANSFER": an ordinary Transfer Package. `files[]` lists top-level files. Use download_package_files and the standard send-flow tools. Does NOT support directories.',
    '- packageType === "DROPZONE": submitted via a Dropzone. Generally the same supported actions as TRANSFER packages.',
    "Orthogonally, if `files[]` is empty and `packageContainsMessage` is true, the package is message-only — suggest get_package_message rather than download tools regardless of packageType.",
    "Recipient download/view history (\"who downloaded what\") lives in different places by packageType. For WORKSPACE packages, call get_activity_log — this returns the package's Activity Log, the same audit stream visible to a Workspace owner in the SendSafely UI. For TRANSFER and DROPZONE packages, read `recipients[].confirmations` from get_package_information (or get_package_information_from_link); SendSafely surfaces this same data to senders as the recipient's \"Recipient History\". Do NOT call get_activity_log for non-workspace packages — it returns nothing useful.",
    "Whenever you inspect a received or sent package, check `packageContainsMessage`. If a secure message exists, TELL the user the package contains one and ask whether they want it decrypted — secure messages are easy to miss and are often the primary payload. Do NOT call get_package_message on your own initiative: decrypting exposes the plaintext message to the AI assistant, so wait for the user to ask for the message before retrieving it.",
    "If a downstream call fails with \"No cached secrets for packageId '...'\" and the user has a registered Trusted Device (private key in the keystore), you do not need the user to paste the keyed link — offer the Trusted Device unlock path instead. Calling get_package_keycode will prompt the user to approve using the Trusted Device for that package; on approval it caches the Keycode so you can retry the original call. Fall back to requesting the keyed link (get_package_information_from_link) if the user declines the unlock or Trusted Device unlock is unavailable or fails.",
].join("\n");
